==============================================================================
READING (same session)
==============================================================================
A* / A0 = 54.0, 53.6, 53.9, 54.2, 54.1, 54.2 across every run -- the
crossover sits at a FIXED GROWTH FACTOR of ~54x the initial amplitude
(ln 54 = 4.0: four e-folds), independent of A0 (factor 4 tested, power
+1.00) and, per NU_CURVE.out, independent of nu across two decades at
A0=100. The invariant is not an amplitude and not a viscous scale: it is
a dynamical AGE of the collapse. This retroactively explains the
nu-independence of the band -- the depletion turns on when the collapse
reaches a fixed stage of its growth, and any nu > 0 in the tested range
then inverts the geometry with the same exponent.

CAVEAT OWNED: the two sweeps are one-axis each (nu swept at A0=100; A0
swept at nu=1e-4). The cross term -- an A0 sweep at a second nu -- is
untested; the "fixed growth factor for all (nu, A0)" reading merges the
sweeps and needs that one cheap test. Estimator health: k-drift 0.9-2.1%,
cross-grid <1% -- the split-free zero-crossing A* passes the invariance
test its chord predecessor failed.

